{"cuisine/brazilian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/brazilian-cuisine","n":"Brazilian cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Brazil","intro":[["Brazilian cuisine  is the set of cooking practices and traditions of Brazil, and is characterized by African, European, Amerindian and Asian (Levantine, Japanese, and most recently, Chinese) influences. It varies greatly by region, reflecting the country's mix of native and immigrant populations, and its continental size as well. This has created a national cuisine marked by the preservation of regional differences."],["Ingredients first used by native peoples in Brazil include cashews, cassava, pineapple, guaraná, açaí, cumaru, and tucupi. From there, the many waves of immigrants brought some of their typical dishes, replacing missing ingredients with local equivalents. For instance, the European immigrants (primarily from Portugal, Italy, Spain, Germany, Netherlands, Poland, and Ukraine), were accustomed to a wheat-based diet, and introduced wine, leafy vegetables, and dairy products into Brazilian cuisine. When potatoes were not available, they discovered how to use the native sweet manioc as a replacement. Enslaved Africans also had a role in developing Brazilian cuisine, especially in the coastal states. The foreign influence extended to later migratory waves; Japanese immigrants brought most of the food items that Brazilians associate with Asian cuisine today, and introduced large-scale aviaries well into the 20th century."],["The most visible regional cuisines belong to the states of Minas Gerais and Bahia. Minas Gerais cuisine has European influence in delicacies and dairy products such as feijão tropeiro, pão de queijo and Minas cheese, and Bahian cuisine due to the presence of African delicacies such as Acarajé, Abará and vatapá."],["Root vegetables such as manioc (locally known as mandioca, aipim or macaxeira, among other names), yams, and fruit like açaí, cupuaçu, mango, papaya, guava, orange, passion fruit, pineapple, and hog plum are among the local ingredients used in cooking."],["Some typical dishes are feijoada, considered the country's national dish, and regional foods such as beiju, feijão tropeiro, vatapá, moqueca capixaba, polenta (from Italian cuisine) and acarajé (from African cuisine). There is also caruru, which consists of okra, onion, dried shrimp, and toasted nuts (peanuts or cashews), cooked with palm oil until a spread-like consistency is reached; moqueca baiana, consisting of slow-cooked fish in palm oil and coconut milk, tomatoes, bell peppers, onions, garlic and topped with cilantro."],["The national beverage is coffee, while cachaça is Brazil's native liquor. Cachaça is distilled from fermented sugar cane must, and is the main ingredient in the national cocktail, caipirinha."],["Pães de queijo and salgadinhos  such as pastéis, coxinhas, risoles and quibe are common finger food items."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"brazilian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brazilian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":19,"links":[["Arabu","dish/arabu"],["Arroz carreteiro","dish/arroz-carreteiro"],["Bauru (sandwich)","dish/bauru-sandwich"],["Beijinho","dish/beijinho"],["Beirute","dish/beirute"],["Bobó de camarão","dish/bobo-de-camarao"],["Bockwurst","dish/bockwurst"],["Bolo de fubá","dish/bolo-de-fuba"],["Brigadeiro","dish/brigadeiro"],["Canja de galinha","dish/canja-de-galinha"],["Coxinha","dish/coxinha"],["Feijoada","dish/feijoada"],["Maniçoba","dish/manicoba"],["Manuê","dish/manue"],["Marmelada de Santa Luzia","dish/marmelada-de-santa-luzia"],["Pão na chapa","dish/pao-na-chapa"],["Pie","dish/pie"],["Sobá","dish/soba"],["Xis (sandwich)","dish/xis-sandwich"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Olivia's Cuisine","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"oliviascuisine.com","note":"Brazilian-born Olivia Mesquita's blog, with a large section of Brazilian classics.","url":"https://oliviascuisine.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Easy and Delish","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"easyanddelish.com","note":"Brazilian recipe collection from a Brazilian-born home cook based in the US.","url":"https://www.easyanddelish.com/easy-brazilian-recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Brazil: The Cookbook","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Josimar Melo","note":"Broad survey of Brazilian home cooking by a São Paulo food critic, published by Phaidon.","url":"https://www.phaidon.com/en-us/products/brazil-the-cookbook","suggested":true},{"name":"Manu: Recipes and Stories from My Brazil","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Manoella Buffara","note":"Chef of Curitiba's Manu restaurant on the food of southern Brazil.","url":"https://www.phaidon.com/en-us/products/manu-recipes-and-stories-from-my-brazil","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Manioc","ingredient/manioc"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Condensed milk","ingredient/condensed-milk"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Broth","ingredient/broth"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Carne-seca","ingredient/carne-seca"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Linguiça","ingredient/linguica"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Parsley","ingredient/parsley"],["Bun","ingredient/bun"],["Mozzarella","ingredient/mozzarella"],["Pickled cucumber","ingredient/pickled-cucumber"],["Pão francês","ingredient/pao-frances"],["Roast beef","ingredient/roast-beef"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"],["Coconut","ingredient/coconut"],["Pita","ingredient/pita"],["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/8/84/Feijoada_01.jpg","w":500,"h":392,"by":"Unknown author","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Feijoada_01.jpg"}},"cuisine/filipino-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/filipino-cuisine","n":"Filipino cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Philippines","intro":[["Filipino cuisine includes the food traditions of more than a hundred ethnolinguistic groups across the Philippine archipelago. Most widely known Filipino dishes come from the culinary traditions of groups such as the Ilocano, Pangasinan, Kapampangan, Tagalog, Bicolano, Visayan, Chavacano, and Maranao communities. The dishes associated with these groups evolved over the centuries from a largely indigenous (largely Austronesian) base shared with maritime Southeast Asia with varied influences from Chinese, Spanish, and American cuisines, in line with the major waves of influence that had enriched the cultures of the archipelago, and adapted using indigenous ingredients to meet local preferences."],["Dishes range from a simple meal of fried salted fish and rice to curries, paellas, and cozidos of Iberian origin made for fiestas. Popular dishes include lechón (whole roasted pig), longganisa (Philippine sausage), tapa (cured beef), torta (omelette), adobo (vinegar and soy sauce-based stew), kaldereta (meat stewed in tomato sauce and liver paste), mechado (larded beef in soy and tomato sauce), pochero (beef and bananas in tomato sauce), afritada (chicken or beef and vegetables simmered in tomato sauce), kare-kare (oxtail and vegetables cooked in peanut sauce), pinakbet (kabocha squash, eggplant, beans, okra, bitter melon, and tomato stew flavored with shrimp paste), sinigang (meat or seafood with vegetables in sour broth), pancit (noodles), and lumpia (fresh or fried spring rolls)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"filipino-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Filipino_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":18,"links":[["Adobo (Filipino dish)","dish/adobo-filipino-dish"],["Aparon","dish/aparon"],["Banana ketchup","dish/banana-ketchup"],["Champorado","dish/champorado"],["Filipino barbecue","dish/filipino-barbecue"],["Food for the gods","dish/food-for-the-gods"],["Isaw","dish/isaw"],["Kadyos, baboy, kag langka","dish/kadyos-baboy-kag-langka"],["Kadyos, manok, kag ubad","dish/kadyos-manok-kag-ubad"],["Kumukunsi","dish/kumukunsi"],["Mango float","dish/mango-float"],["Palapa (condiment)","dish/palapa-condiment"],["Pastil","dish/pastil"],["Piaparan","dish/piaparan"],["Pilipit","dish/pilipit"],["Piyanggang manok","dish/piyanggang-manok"],["Sushi bake","dish/sushi-bake"],["Tortang talong","dish/tortang-talong"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Panlasang Pinoy","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"panlasangpinoy.com","note":"Vanjo Merano's large library of Filipino recipes and cooking videos.","url":"https://panlasangpinoy.com/filipino-food/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Kawaling Pinoy","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"kawalingpinoy.com","note":"Lalaine Manalo's blog of Filipino home cooking.","url":"https://www.kawalingpinoy.com/about/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"I Am a Filipino: And This Is How We Cook","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Nicole Ponseca and Miguel Trinidad, 2018","note":"Filipino classics and their history from the team behind Maharlika in New York.","url":"https://hachettebookgroup.com/titles/nicole-ponseca/i-am-a-filipino/9781579657673","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781579657673"},{"name":"Filipinx: Heritage Recipes from the Diaspora","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Angela Dimayuga and Ligaya Mishan, 2021","note":"Filipino-American chef's take on heritage dishes.","url":"https://www.abramsbooks.com/product/filipinx_9781419750380/","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781419750380"}]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["Glutinous rice","ingredient/glutinous-rice"],["Garcinia binucao","ingredient/garcinia-binucao"],["Pigeon pea","ingredient/pigeon-pea"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Turmeric","ingredient/turmeric"],["Bay leaf","ingredient/bay-leaf"],["Black pepper","ingredient/black-pepper"],["Soy sauce","ingredient/soy-sauce"],["Vinegar","ingredient/vinegar"],["Banana","ingredient/banana"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Tabliya","ingredient/tabliya"],["Jackfruit","ingredient/jackfruit"],["Banana pith","ingredient/banana-pith"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Duck egg","ingredient/duck-egg"],["Duck eggs","ingredient/duck-eggs"],["Rice flour","ingredient/rice-flour"],["Carabao mango","ingredient/carabao-mango"],["Condensed milk","ingredient/condensed-milk"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Within Filipino cooking","count":7,"links":[["Filipino Chinese cuisine","cuisine/filipino-chinese-cuisine"],["Filipino-American cuisine","cuisine/filipino-american-cuisine"],["Ilocano cuisine","cuisine/ilocano-cuisine"],["Kapampangan cuisine","cuisine/kapampangan-cuisine"],["Mindanao cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine-mindanao"],["Tagalog cuisine","cuisine/tagalog-cuisine"],["Visayan cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine-visayas"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/8/88/Philippine_Food.jpg/500px-Philippine_Food.jpg","w":500,"h":225,"by":"Philippine Department of Tourism","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Philippine_Food.jpg"}},"cuisine/tuvaluan-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/tuvaluan-cuisine","n":"Tuvaluan cuisine","c":"#55b8ad","crumb":[["Oceania","cuisines#oceania"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Tuvalu","intro":[["The cuisine of Tuvalu, a state in the Central Pacific (Oceania), is based on the staple of coconut and the many species of fish found in the ocean and the lagoons of the atolls of Tuvalu. Pulaka (Cyrtosperma merkusii), or \"swamp taro\", is an important source of carbohydrates. Rice now forms an important part of the diet. Coconut is used in different forms with coconut water, coconut milk and the flesh of the coconut being used to flavour dishes. Various desserts made on the islands include coconut and coconut milk, instead of animal milk."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"tuvaluan-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tuvaluan_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Eat Pacific: The Pacific Island Food Revolution Cookbook","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Robert Oliver (ed.), 2024","note":"Pacific Island Food Revolution cookbook with a Tuvalu section.","url":"https://masseypress.ac.nz/books/eat-pacific","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781991016805"}]}],"cont":"Oceanic cuisine","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/3/34/Tuvalu_Inaba-14.jpg/500px-Tuvalu_Inaba-14.jpg","w":500,"h":753,"by":"INABA Tomoaki","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tuvalu_Inaba-14.jpg"}},"ingredient/cardamom":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/cardamom","n":"Cardamom","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 14 dishes across 23 cuisines","intro":[["Cardamom (), sometimes cardamon or cardamum, is a spice made from the seeds of several plants in the genera Elettaria and Amomum in the family Zingiberaceae. Both genera are native to the Indian subcontinent and Indonesia. They are recognized by their small seed pods: triangular in cross-section and spindle-shaped, with a thin, papery outer shell and small, black seeds; Elettaria pods are light green and smaller, while Amomum pods are dark brown and larger."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-cardamom"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.or