#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Indonesian cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Ayam goreng Kalasan is an Indonesian dish consisting of fried chicken topped with kremesan, crisp bits of fried batter. The dish originates from the area surrounding the Kalasan Temple in Sleman, Yogyakarta, and has since spread nationwide, being considered a popular iteration of the ubiquitous ayam goreng."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-ayam-goreng-kalasan"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ayam_goreng_kalasan"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":8,"links":[["Ayam goreng","ingredient/ayam-goreng"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Coconut water","ingredient/coconut-water"],["Galangal","ingredient/galangal"],["Palm sugar","ingredient/palm-sugar"],["Shrimp paste","ingredient/shrimp-paste"],["Sweet soy sauce","ingredient/sweet-soy-sauce"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Daily Cooking Quest","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"dailycookingquest.com","note":"Indonesian home cook Anita's blog with a large archive of Indonesian recipes.","url":"https://dailycookingquest.com/cuisines/indonesian.html","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Indonesian cuisine","links":[["Arem-arem","dish/arem-arem"],["Arsik","dish/arsik"],["Asam pedas","dish/asam-pedas"],["Ayam goreng","dish/ayam-goreng"],["Ayam panggang","dish/ayam-panggang"],["Ayam Taliwang","dish/ayam-taliwang"],["Bajigur","dish/bajigur"],["Bakakak hayam","dish/bakakak-hayam"],["Bakpia","dish/bakpia"],["Bakwan","dish/bakwan"],["Bekasang","dish/bekasang"],["Betutu","dish/betutu"],["Bika ambon","dish/bika-ambon"],["Binte biluhuta","dish/binte-biluhuta"],["Bir pletok","dish/bir-pletok"],["Bolu kukus","dish/bolu-kukus"],["Botok","dish/botok"],["Brem","dish/brem"],["Bubur kacang hijau","dish/bubur-kacang-hijau"],["Bubur ketan hitam","dish/bubur-ketan-hitam"],["Bubur pedas","dish/bubur-pedas"],["Buntil","dish/buntil"],["Burgo (food)","dish/burgo-food"],["Cendol","dish/cendol"],["Cenil","dish/cenil"],["Cilok","dish/cilok"],["Cimpa","dish/cimpa"],["Corn fritter","dish/corn-fritter"],["Coto makassar","dish/coto-makassar"],["Dadar gulung","dish/dadar-gulung"],["Dadiah","dish/dadiah"],["Dengke mas naniura","dish/dengke-mas-naniura"],["Empal gentong","dish/empal-gentong"],["Emping","dish/emping"],["Es buah","dish/es-buah"],["Es doger","dish/es-doger"],["Es goyobod","dish/es-goyobod"],["Gado-gado","dish/gado-gado"],["Geplak","dish/geplak"],["Gepuk","dish/gepuk"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/9/9d/Ayam_goreng_kalasan.JPG/500px-Ayam_goreng_kalasan.JPG","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Midori","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ayam_goreng_kalasan.JPG"}},"dish/beer-cake":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/beer-cake","n":"Beer cake","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from American cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Beer cake is a cake prepared using beer as a primary ingredient, and other typical cake ingredients. Chocolate beer cakes may include stout and chocolate stout beer, and some gingerbread cakes include beer in their preparation. The Rabha tribe in India uses a type of beer cake prepared with rice to create rice beer and fotika, a liquor. Beer cake variations exist, such as root beer cake."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-beer-cake"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beer_cake"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Beer","ingredient/beer"]]},{"h":"More from American cuisine","links":[["Angel food cake","dish/angel-food-cake"],["Banana bread","dish/banana-bread"],["Banana pudding","dish/banana-pudding"],["Barbecue sauce","dish/barbecue-sauce"],["Better than sex cake","dish/better-than-sex-cake"],["Blini","dish/blini"],["Blooming onion","dish/blooming-onion"],["Brown Betty (dessert)","dish/brown-betty-dessert"],["Brunswick stew","dish/brunswick-stew"],["Buffalo wing","dish/buffalo-wing"],["Butter cake","dish/butter-cake"],["Caesar salad","dish/caesar-salad"],["Caramel apple","dish/caramel-apple"],["Carbonara","dish/carbonara"],["Cheesesteak","dish/cheesesteak"],["Chicago-style hot dog","dish/chicago-style-hot-dog"],["Chicken nugget","dish/chicken-nugget"],["Chiffon cake","dish/chiffon-cake"],["Chili con carne","dish/chili-con-carne"],["Chimichanga","dish/chimichanga"],["Chow-chow (food)","dish/chow-chow-food"],["Chowder","dish/chowder"],["Cioppino","dish/cioppino"],["Clam chowder","dish/clam-chowder"],["Clam dip","dish/clam-dip"],["Club sandwich","dish/club-sandwich"],["Cobb salad","dish/cobb-salad"],["Cobbler (food)","dish/cobbler-food"],["Cook's Corner","dish/cook-s-corner"],["Crab cake","dish/crab-cake"],["Croffle","dish/croffle"],["Dagwood Bumstead","dish/dagwood-bumstead"],["Dagwood sandwich","dish/dagwood-sandwich"],["Deep-dish pizza","dish/deep-dish-pizza"],["Desperation pie","dish/desperation-pie"],["Dirty rice","dish/dirty-rice"],["Donut hole","dish/donut-hole"],["Egg in the basket","dish/egg-in-the-basket"],["Fish and chips","dish/fish-and-chips"],["Fritter","dish/fritter"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/7/79/Chocolate_Stout_Cake.jpg/500px-Chocolate_Stout_Cake.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Peter Alfred Hess from United States","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chocolate_Stout_Cake.jpg"}},"ingredient/fondant":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/fondant","n":"Fondant","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 3 cuisines","intro":[["Fondant icing, also commonly just called fondant, is an icing used to decorate or sculpt cakes and pastries. It is made from sugar, water, gelatin, vegetable oil or shortening, and glycerol. It does not have the texture of most icings; rolled fondant is akin to modelling clay, while poured fondant is a thick liquid. The flavor and texture are generally considered a weak point, as it is sweet and chalky.  It is generally chosen for its appearance rather than flavor."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-fondant"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fondant_icing"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine",[["Mille-feuille","dish/mille-feuille"]]],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine",[["Mille-feuille","dish/mille-feuille"]]],["Swiss cuisine","cuisine/swiss-cuisine",[["Carac (pastry)","dish/carac-pastry"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Chocolate","ingredient/chocolate"],["Ganache","ingredient/ganache"],["Icing","ingredient/icing"],["Pie crust","ingredient/pie-crust"],["Custard","ingredient/custard"],["Powdered sugar","ingredient/powdered-sugar"],["Puff pastry","ingredient/puff-pastry"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/d/da/Cakeinwhitesatin-1.jpg","w":500,"h":638,"by":"I, Michael Prudhomme","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cakeinwhitesatin-1.jpg"}},"dish/ema-datshi":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/ema-datshi","n":"Ema datshi","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Bhutanese cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Ema datshi is a spicy Bhutanese stew made from hot chili peppers and cheese. It is among the most famous dishes in Bhutanese cuisine, recognized as the national dish of the country.  Over the years, ema datshi, which has its roots in Tibetan culinary traditions, has undergone various changes to suit the preference of local Bhutanese taste."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-ema-datshi"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ema_datshi"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Bhutanese cuisine","cuisine/bhutanese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Datshi","ingredient/datshi"]]},{"h":"More from Bhutanese cuisine","links":[["Momo (food)","dish/momo-food"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/b/b6/Bhutanese_hemadatsi.jpg/500px-Bhutanese_hemadatsi.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Bhutanese_hemadatsi_and_rice.JPG: ShashiBellamkonda derivative work: Thejinan (talk)","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bhutanese_hemadatsi.jpg"}},"dish/embutido":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/embutido","n":"Embutido","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 4 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Embutido (Spanish and Brazilian Portuguese), enchido (European Portuguese), and embotit (Catalan) are generic terms for cured ground meat products. The dictionary of the Royal Spanish Academy defines it as \"intestine stuffed with minced meat, mainly pork; intestine stuffed with diverse ingredients\" (the Spanish word comes from the verb embutir, meaning 'to stuff'). The term often applies to any of the many varieties of cured, dry sausages found in the cuisines of Iberia and the former Spanish and Portuguese colonies."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-embutido"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Embutido"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Asturian cuisine","cuisine/asturian-cuisine"],["Galician cuisine","cuisine/galician-cuisine"],["Portuguese cuisine","cuisine/portuguese-cuisine"],["Spanish cuisine","cuisine/spanish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Food From Portugal","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"foodfromportugal.com","note":"Portuguese-run recipe site and video channel of traditional Portuguese dishes.","url":"https://www.foodfromportugal.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"site"},{"name":"Spain on a Fork","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"spainonafork.com","note":"Albert Bevia's video channel and site of traditional and everyday Spanish recipes.","url":"https://spainonafork.com/welcome2/","suggested":true,"kind":"youtube"}]},{"h":"More from Asturian cuisine","links":[["Tocino de cielo","dish/tocino-de-cielo"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/c/ca/Embutidos_frescos.JPG/500px-Embutidos_frescos.JPG","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Juan Emilio Prades Bel","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Embutidos_frescos.JPG"}},"ingredient/hay":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/hay","n":"Hay","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Hay is grass, legumes, or other herbaceous plants that have been cut and dried to be stored for use as animal fodder, either for large grazing animals raised as livestock, such as cattle, horses, goats, and sheep, or for smaller domesticated animals such as rabbits and guinea pigs. Pigs can eat hay, but they do not digest it as efficiently as herbivores do."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-hay"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hay"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine",[["Foin de Crau","dish/foin-de-crau"]]],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine",[["Foin de Crau","dish/foin-de-crau"]]]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/4/4c/Roundbale1.jpg/500px-Roundbale1.jpg","w":500,"h":342,"by":"I, Montanabw","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roundbale1.jpg"}},"dish/jelly-bean":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/jelly-bean","n":"Jelly bean","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from American cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Jelly beans are small, sometimes medium sized bean-shaped sugar candies with soft candy shells and thick gel interiors (see gelatin and jelly). The confection is primarily made of sugar and sold in a wide variety of colors and wacky flavours."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-jelly-bean"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jelly_bean"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]]},{"h":"More from American cuisine","links":[["Angel food cake","dish/angel-food-cake"],["Banana bread","dish/banana-bread"],["Banana pudding","dish/banana-pudding"],["Barbecue sauce","dish/barbecue-sauce"],["Beer cake","dish/beer-cake"],["Better than sex cake","dish/better-than-sex-cake"],["Blini","dish/blini"],["Blooming onion","dish/blooming-onion"],["Brown Betty (dessert)","dish/brown-betty-dessert"],["Brunswick stew","dish/brunswick-stew"],["Buffalo wing","dish/buffalo-wing"],["Butter cake","dish/butter-cake"],["Caesar salad","dish/caesar-salad"],["Caramel apple","dish/caramel-apple"],["Carbonara","dish/carbonara"],["Cheesesteak","dish/cheesesteak"],["Chicago-style hot dog","dish/chicago-style-hot-dog"],["Chicken nugget","dish/chicken-nugget"],["Chiffon cake","dish/chiffon-cake"],["Chili con carne","dish/chili-con-carne"],["Chimichanga","dish/chimichanga"],["Chow-chow (food)","dish/chow-chow-food"],["Chowder","dish/chowder"],["Cioppino","dish/cioppino"],["Clam chowder","dish/clam-chowder"],["Clam dip","dish/clam-dip"],["Club sandwich","dish/club-sandwich"],["Cobb salad","dish/cobb-salad"],["Cobbler (food)","dish/cobbler-food"],["Cook's Corner","dish/cook-s-corner"],["Crab cake","dish/crab-cake"],["Croffle","dish/croffle"],["Dagwood Bumstead","dish/dagwood-bumstead"],["Dagwood sandwich","dish/dagwood-sandwich"],["Deep-dish pizza","dish/deep-dish-pizza"],["Desperation pie","dish/desperation-pie"],["Dirty rice","dish/dirty-rice"],["Donut hole","dish/donut-hole"],["Egg in the basket","dish/egg-in-the-basket"],["Fish and chips","dish/fish-and-chips"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/1/14/JellyBellyBeans.jpg/500px-JellyBellyBeans.jpg","w":500,"h":389,"by":"The original uploader was Brandon Dilbeck at English Wikipedia.","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JellyBellyBeans.jpg"}},"dish/lo-bah-png":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/lo-bah-png","n":"Ló͘-bah-pn̄g","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Taiwan cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Ló͘-bah-pn̄g, also called bah-sò-pn̄g or Lou-rou-fan in southern Taiwan, or translated to minced pork rice in English, is a rice dish that is commonly seen throughout Taiwan and Southern Fujian, China. The flavor may vary from one region to another, but the basic ingredients remain the same: lu rou pork marinated and boiled in soy sauce served on top of steamed rice. It is a type of gaifan dish."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-lo-bah-png"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/L%C3%B3%CD%98-bah-pn%CC%84g"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Taiwan cuisine","cuisine/taiwan-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":6,"links":[["Lurou","ingredient/lurou"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Rice wine","ingredient/rice-wine"],["Soy sauce","ingredient/soy-sauce"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Cooking in Chinglish","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"cookinginchinglish.com","note":"Taiwanese home recipes from Edwina, who grew up in Taiwan.","url":"https://cookinginchinglish.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Tiny Urban Kitchen: Taiwanese Recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"tinyurbankitchen.com","note":"Small set of Taiwanese recipes from Jen Che, a Taiwanese American blogger in Hong Kong.","url":"https://tinyurbankitchen.com/taiwanese-recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Taiwan cuisine","links":[["Ageh (food)","dish/ageh-food"],["Alivongvong","dish/alivongvong"],["Apple bread","dish/apple-bread"],["Bah-uân","dish/bah-uan"],["Braised pork rice","dish/braised-pork-rice"],["Cinavu","dish/cinavu"],["Coffin bread","dish/coffin-bread"],["Doujiang","dish/doujiang"],["Egg yolk pastry","dish/egg-yolk-pastry"],["Iron egg","dish/iron-egg"],["Kiâm-piánn","dish/kiam-piann"],["Mongolian barbecue","dish/mongolian-barbecue"],["Peanut ice cream roll","dish/peanut-ice-cream-roll"],["Penglai rice","dish/penglai-rice"],["Pineapple cake","dish/pineapple-cake"],["Pineapple shrimp balls","dish/pineapple-shrimp-balls"],["Pork ball","dish/pork-ball"],["Rechao","dish/rechao"],["Shuangbaotai","dish/shuangbaotai"],["Small sausage in large sausage","dish/small-sausage-in-large-sausage"],["Taiwanese beef noodle soup","dish/taiwanese-beef-noodle-soup"],["Taiwanese fried chicken","dish/taiwanese-fried-chicken"],["Taiwanese fried chicken cutlet","dish/taiwanese-fried-chicken-cutlet"],["Tàⁿ-á-mī","dish/ta-a-mi"],["Tea egg","dish/tea-egg"],["Ti-hueh-kué","dish/ti-hueh-kue"],["Tshik-á-mī","dish/tshik-a-mi"],["Tube rice pudding","dish/tube-rice-pudding"],["Xiaolongbao","dish/xiaolongbao"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/7/75/Cubed_Pork_with_Rice.jpg/500px-Cubed_Pork_with_Rice.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"kawanet","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cubed_Pork_with_Rice.jpg"}},"dish/rice-noodle-roll":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/rice-noodle-roll","n":"Rice noodle roll","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 3 cuisines","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Rice noodle rolls, also known as steamed rice rolls, cheung fun, and as look funn or look fun in Hawaii, is a Cantonese dish originating from Guangdong Province in southern China. It is commonly served as either a snack, small meal or variety of dim sum. It is a thin roll made from a wide strip of shahe fen (rice noodles), filled with shrimp, beef, vegetables, or other ingredients. Seasoned soy sauce – sometimes with siu mei drippings – is poured over the dish upon serving. When plain and made without filling, the rice noodle is also known as jyu cheung fun or  chee cheong fun (豬腸粉), literally \"pork sausage noodle, pork intestine noodle\", a reference to its resemblance to a pig's intestines. There is no official recording of the history of rice noodle rolls; most cookbooks claim that it was first made in the 1930s. In Guangzhou, Guangdong Province, people called the dish la"]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-rice-noodle-roll"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rice_noodle_roll"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Cantonese cuisine","cuisine/cantonese-cuisine"],["Hong Kong cuisine","cuisine/hong-kong-cuisine"],["Thai cuisine","cuisine/thai-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Rice noodles","ingredient/rice-noodles"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Made With Lau","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"madewithlau.com","note":"Family-run channel and site teaching Cantonese recipes from retired chef Daddy Lau.","url":"https://madewithlau.com","suggested":true,"kind":"youtube"},{"name":"Hot Thai Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"hot-thai-kitchen.com","note":"Pailin Chongchitnant's recipes and videos, with background on technique and ingredients.","url":"https://hot-thai-kitchen.com","suggested":true,"kind":"youtube"},{"name":"SheSimmers","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"shesimmers.com","note":"Bangkok-born writer Leela Punyaratabandhu's blog on Thai home cooking.","url":"https://shesimmers.com/who-is-shesimmers","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Cantonese cuisine","links":[["Cantonese restaurant","dish/cantonese-restaurant"],["Char siu","dish/char-siu"],["Har gow","dish/har-gow"],["Hong dou tang","dish/hong-dou-tang"],["Lap cheong","dish/lap-cheong"],["Siu mei","dish/siu-mei"],["Sweet and sour pork","dish/sweet-and-sour-pork"],["Turnip cake","dish/turnip-cake"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/a/a9/GD_Rice_Product_1.JPG/500px-GD_Rice_Product_1.JPG","w":500,"h":375,"by":"No machine-readable author provided. GS417~commonswiki assumed (based on copyright…","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.5","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GD_Rice_Product_1.JPG"}},"ingredient/milk-chocolate":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/milk-chocolate","n":"Milk chocolate","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Milk chocolate is a form of solid chocolate containing cocoa, sugar and milk. It is the most consumed type of chocolate, and is used in a wide diversity of bars and other confectionery products. Although rich in cocoa butter, milk chocolate contains a smaller amount of cocoa mass than dark chocolate does, and (as with white chocolate) contains milk solids. While its taste has been key to its popularity, milk chocolate was historically promoted as a healthy food, particularly for children."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-milk-chocolate"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Milk_chocolate"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine",[["Stracciatella (ice cream)","dish/stracciatella-ice-cream"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Chocolate","ingredient/chocolate"],["Cream","ingredient/cream"],["Egg yolk","ingredient/egg-yolk"],["Gelatin","ingredient/gelatin"],["Ice cream","ingredient/ice-cream"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Smetana","ingredient/smetana"],["Sodium alginate","ingredient/sodium-alginate"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]]}]},"ingredient/quark-dairy-product":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/quark-dairy-product","n":"Quark (dairy product)","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 3 cuisines","intro":[["Quark or quarg (sometimes translated as curd cheese) is a type of fresh dairy product made from milk. The milk is soured, usually by adding lactic acid bacteria cultures, and strained once the desired curdling is achieved. It can be classified as fresh acid-set cheese. Traditional quark can be made without rennet, but in modern dairies small quantities of rennet are typically added. It is soft, white and unaged, and usually has no salt added."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-quark-dairy-product"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quark_(dairy_product)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["Belarusian cuisine","cuisine/belarusian-cuisine",[["Syrniki","dish/syrniki"]]],["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine",[["Syrniki","dish/syrniki"]]],["Ukrainian cuisine","cuisine/ukrainian-cuisine",[["Syrniki","dish/syrniki"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Cottage cheese","ingredient/cottage-cheese"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Raisin","ingredient/raisin"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Tvorog","ingredient/tvorog"],["Vanilla extract","ingredient/vanilla-extract"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/7/7b/Skimmed_milk_quark_on_spoon.jpg/500px-Skimmed_milk_quark_on_spoon.jpg","w":500,"h":400,"by":"SKopp","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skimmed_milk_quark_on_spoon.jpg"}},"dish/muktuk":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/muktuk","n":"Muktuk","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 3 cuisines","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Muktuk is whale skin with its layer of blubber, eaten raw, frozen, or boiled, often cut into small cubes. In Iñupiaq whaling communities, a successful hunt is shared across the whole town."]],"src":"note","sources":[],"actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-muktuk"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Muktuk"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Iñupiaq foodways","cuisine/inupiaq-foodways"],["St. Lawrence Island Yupik foodways","cuisine/st-lawrence-island-yupik-foodways"],["Inuit cuisine","cuisine/inuit-diet"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Whale","ingredient/whale-meat"]]},{"h":"More from Iñupiaq foodways","links":[["Akutaq","dish/akutaq"]]}]}}